From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: office <office@localhost>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] layout: table column widths as Word lays them out (spanning cells, legacy compatibility modes, tables past the column)

Owner finding (usability test 2026-10-29): tables made in Word lose their layout. Measured against
Word 16's own PDF of 29 Word-made tables (docs/phase4/tables.md in the product repo); three causes in
layout_table, all in AutoFit (w:tblLayout autofit, ECMA-376 Part 1 17.4: the preferred widths of the
table and cells, then the cell contents, give the final column widths):

- column_content: a cell spanning grid columns (w:gridSpan) needed an even share of its longest word
  from every column it spans, so a narrow column under a merged cell (forms, timesheets, rows with
  their own cell boundaries) grew past its w:gridCol width and its neighbours shrank. Each column now
  needs its share in proportion to its grid width, so the columns grow only when together they are
  too narrow for the cell. Without a grid the even share stays.
- In compatibility mode 14 and below (w:compatSetting compatibilityMode, [MS-DOCX]) Word hangs the
  table out by its cell margins on both sides, so percentage (w:tblW/w:tcW type pct) and AutoFit
  widths are taken of the text column plus the first cell's left and the table's right cell margin:
  Word writes a 9571 tw grid for a 100% table in a 9355 tw column in mode 14, 9355 in mode 15.
- A table with a preferred width of its own (w:tblW dxa or pct, which Word writes once columns are
  sized by hand) is no longer squeezed into the column when it is over 1.5 times as wide: Word lets it
  run past the margin and the page edge. Tables with w:tblW auto keep the old rule.

Tests: a_cell_spanning_a_narrow_column_keeps_the_grid,
legacy_mode_percentage_tables_take_the_cell_margins_too,
a_preferred_table_width_wider_than_the_column_stands (each fails without the change).
